  "cues": [
   [
    0.117,
    "Put the two routes side by side."
   ],
   [
    2.188,
    "Both begin with glycolysis."
   ],
   [
    4.258,
    "In fermentation, the final electron acceptor is an organic molecule, pyruvate or acetaldehyde."
   ],
   [
    11.12,
    "In aerobic respiration it is oxygen."
   ],
   [
    13.828,
    "Fermentation makes two A T P from one glucose."
   ],
   [
    18.047,
    "Aerobic respiration makes about thirty to thirty-two."
   ],
   [
    21.693,
    "The reason is simple."
   ],
   [
    23.099,
    "Fermentation stops after glycolysis, and so all the energy still locked in lactate and ethanol is never harvested."
   ],
   [
    30.911,
    "That is also why a fermenting cell must burn through far more glucose to get the same A T P."
   ],
   [
    37.279,
    "For example, to match one glucose's worth of respiration, a fermenting cell needs roughly fifteen glucose molecules, an estimate, not a slide figure."
   ]
  ]
 },
